# `__modname__` command group

`weld add command __modname__` generated this package. weld writes it once and
records every file in `weld.json`; it does not regenerate them, so your edits
survive later `weld add` commands.

## What it is

An independent root command group that starts no long-running work. `NewCommand`
returns the `__modname__` command; with no subcommands a bare
`__name__ __modname__` shows help.

Generating the command also installed `loom` (and the `config` it requires), so
the group resolves its dependencies from the command-specific graph in
`internal/di/__modname___graph.go`. That graph includes `commonModule`, the
shared provider set `di.go` declares (configuration, logger and the installed
db/redis/mail/storage bindings), plus the `Deps` root, and Loom prunes every
provider a subcommand does not consume, so the group never constructs the HTTP
server, the scheduler or a database pool.

## How it is wired

`internal/app/__modname___command.go` registers the group through
`app.RegisterCommand` and delegates `Build` to `di.Init__ModName__`. A real
subcommand calls `g.resolve`, which builds and starts the graph for that one
invocation and stops it within a bounded deadline; help, `version` and every
unrelated command never resolve it.

To inject a dependency: add a field to `Deps` and the matching parameter to
`NewDeps`. The shared providers are already declared through `commonModule`, so
no graph edit is needed — a capability installed after this command still
reaches it. `internal/di/loom_gen.go` is generated by Loom; never edit it.

## Adding behaviour

Add subcommands in `command.go` with `cmd.AddCommand`. Each real subcommand
should resolve the graph lazily through `g.resolve`, for example:

```go
RunE: func(cmd *cobra.Command, args []string) error {
    return g.resolve(cmd.Context(), func(ctx context.Context, deps *Deps) error {
        // use deps, then return nil
        return nil
    })
}
```
